    "notes": [
      "The staircase is legible. Far view sits at columns 1–3, rows 5–6. Middle view sits at columns 4–7, rows 6–9. Near view sits at columns 8–12, rows 7–12. Each step drops one row, shifts one block right and grows 2→4→6 rows. Keep this structure.",
      "The idea is weakened by the middle view, which is image 1 at 2×. It is a crop of the far view, not a new point on the path. Replace columns 4–7, rows 6–9 with a genuine mid-path photograph so the three modules read as three positions on the approach.",
      "The widths grow 3→4→5 columns but the heights grow 2→4→6 rows, so the frames change aspect as well as size. That is fine, but match the crops so the keep's horizon line steps down consistently. In the far view the horizon sits high in rows 5–6. In the near view it falls near the top of row 10. Keep that drop, but set the middle view's horizon around row 7 so it lands midway.",
      "Row 4 is fully empty under the headline across columns 1–12. Columns 8–12, rows 4–6 are empty above the near view. Together this is the intended negative space of the staircase. Leave both empty. Do not fill them.",
      "The 'Aberdeenshire' label in columns 1–3, row 7 floats alone above three empty modules (columns 1–3, rows 8–9). It also repeats the header text. Either remove it, or replace it with a step caption such as 'clifftop path', and add matching captions under the middle view (columns 4–7, top of row 10) and the near view. That would make the approach explicit.",
      "The lead spans columns 1–6, rows 10–11. Its longest line, ending 'about', reaches into column 6, so it sits close to the near view's left edge at column 8. Narrow the lead to columns 1–5 so the rag clears the gutter, or keep it at 6 columns and tighten to a 2-line break.",
      "The body in columns 1–4, row 12 hangs one baseline short of the footer rule. Align its last baseline to the bottom of row 12, matching the near view's bottom edge, so both elements finish on the same line.",
      "Hierarchy runs headline (rows 2–3) → near view (rows 7–12) → lead (rows 10–11), which is correct. The step from lead to body is large. Consider giving the body a 5-column measure so it reads as a deliberate tier rather than a footnote.",
      "The accent #60684F appears only in the tiny label at columns 1–3, row 7, where it is nearly invisible. Carry it into the step captions, or into a thin rule tracing the staircase edges. That would let it echo the clifftop green in the photos.",
      "The near view's warm evening light (columns 8–12) clashes with the cool daylight of the two left frames. Colour-grade all three to one light, or order them so the warmth increases with each step as a deliberate time-of-day progression."
    ],
    "summary": "A clear, well-executed staircase of three frames: each steps one row lower and one block right, growing 2→4→6 rows. The grid logic is sound and the negative space is intentional. The idea is undercut because the middle frame is a crop of the far frame rather than a new viewpoint, and the three photographs don't share light. The accent is barely used, and the lone county label wastes an opportunity to name the steps of the approach. Fix the middle image, add step captions in the accent, and align the body and lead to the photo edges.",
    "mean": 6.6
  },
